# Ambulance Route Optimization System

A mini project that finds the fastest and most efficient route for an
ambulance to reach a patient or hospital, using graph theory and
shortest-path algorithms (Dijkstra's algorithm and A* search), with
traffic-aware edge weights.

The project has three parts, each usable independently:

| Folder | What it is | Tech |
|---|---|---|
| `web/` | An interactive, ready-to-open dispatch console website with a live map, route animation, and traffic simulation | HTML / CSS / JavaScript (no install needed) |
| `backend/` | A REST API implementing the same routing logic, for use from any client | Python (Flask) |
| `c_version/` | A console program demonstrating the core Dijkstra algorithm with an adjacency list + binary heap | C |

All three model the same sample city: 16 locations (3 ambulance depots,
3 hospitals, 10 road junctions) connected by 25 weighted road segments.

---

## 1. Website (`web/`) — start here

This is the main deliverable: a self-contained website, no server or
installation required.

**To run it:** open `web/index.html` directly in any browser, or serve it:

```bash
cd web
python3 -m http.server 8000
# then visit http://localhost:8000
```

**Features:**
- Interactive SVG map of the city road network — junctions, hospitals, and
  ambulance depots, with roads colour-coded by traffic (green/amber/red)
- Pick an origin and destination from dropdowns, **or** click two nodes on
  the map directly
- Two routing modes: **Fastest (traffic-aware)** vs **Shortest distance**
- Dijkstra's algorithm runs live in the browser and animates the computed
  route, with an ambulance marker travelling along the path
- **Simulate Live Traffic** button randomly changes road conditions and
  recomputes the active route, showing how the optimal path adapts
- A route report panel: total distance, ETA, turn-by-turn stops, number of
  junctions passed, and algorithm compute time
- A console log panel narrating what the system is doing (dispatch-software
  style), for demo/presentation purposes
- Fully responsive layout (desktop, tablet, mobile)

No build step, no dependencies, no API keys — everything (graph data +
Dijkstra implementation) lives in `web/script.js`.
